WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let me show you how

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.700
to add cloud endpoints
to an existing app engine

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.710
flexible environment
application.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.460
First of all, let's try running
the application locally.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.270
It is a simple echo
server [? run in ?] go,

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.530
so we can run it with Go Run.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.670
Now we can verify that
everything works correctly,

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.102
sending a post
request with Curl.

00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:19.560
To deploy the
application, we first

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.570
configure the project
at [INAUDIBLE] cloud.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.210
And then we run aedeploy,
gcloud app deploy.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.650
The aedeploy part is needed
only for go applications.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.230
After a short period of time,
the application is deployed,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.900
and we can test it
again using Curl.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.350
Great, let's add some
cloud endpoints now.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.440
We will need an open APS
specification of our API.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.420
In our case, it's
a very simple one.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.150
One single path that
replies to a post request

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.090
with the same
message it receives.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.950
We can now deploy
that specification

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.394
to cloud endpoints with
these gcloud command.

00:00:50.394 --> 00:00:52.310
As a result, we will
receive a cloud endpoints

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:54.830
name and a configuration ID.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.630
We now add those two
values to our app.yaml,

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.420
and we deploy application.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.340
Open API is an open
standard, which

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:03.650
means all the tools
developed for open API

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.920
are now available for you
to use with cloud endpoints.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.650
For instance, if you
get your specification

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.680
and put it in
editor.swagger.io, you

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.320
can't see the autogenerated
documentation, which

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.780
is incredibly useful
for the developers,

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.110
and, also, you can even run
the application directly here

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:24.560
by saying, hello,
running that request,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.970
and getting the response.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:26.990
Great, right?

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.980
Now that we have
deployed our application

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.550
and the corresponding
open API specification,

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:34.220
we get to enjoy
monitoring and logging.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.280
We can fake some traffic and see
how the graphics react to it,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.790
or go see how the logs
appear in real time.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.940
OK, one last thing,
let's secure our API

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.050
so only calls with an API
key will be authorized.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.340
We do not need to
change our code just

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.220
to add a security
definition on our open API

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.390
specification and redeploy.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.090
Let's test it with Curl now.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:59.150
Without the API key,
our request is rejected.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.480
But with the correct API
key, everything works fine.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.660
So there you go, with a
simple open APS specification

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.449
and cloud endpoint, we
get monitoring, logging,

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:10.490
and authentication,
and the possibility

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.520
of using all the tools that
already exist for the open API

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.770
ecosystem.

